**Abstract**  
This study employs detailed axisymmetric radiative transfer models to explore the physical distinctions between two subclasses of proto-planetary nebulae (PPNs): star-obvious low-level elongated PPNs (SOLE PPNs) and dust-prominent longitudinally extended PPNs (DUPLEX PPNs). By focusing on well-studied representatives—HD 161796 (a SOLE PPN) and IRAS 17150-3224 (a DUPLEX PPN)—we aim to disentangle the effects of mass-loss history, inclination angles, and dust composition on their observed properties. Both objects are modeled using a radiative transfer code that incorporates laboratory-derived dust optical constants, enabling a rigorous comparison of their structures and evolutionary states.  

